What is driving secondary battery recycling?
Production scrap availability, established lead-acid collection and increasingly specific recovery obligations support activity. At the same time, uneven feedstock delivery and differences between battery chemistries limit how quickly announced processing capacity can become sustainable throughput.

The IEA identifies a timing gap between battery sales and retired feedstock
In its Global EV Outlook 2026, the International Energy Agency found that lithium-ion recycling currently relies heavily on battery manufacturing scrap. It expects end-of-life batteries to become the larger source only around the mid-2030s, because most vehicles and storage systems deployed in recent years remain in service. The agency also described China as hosting more than 85% of global recycling capacity, a significant difference between industrial capacity and the geography of available waste streams.

This is an important limit on simple growth narratives. A rise in electric-vehicle sales creates a potential future recycling resource but not an equal volume of discarded batteries in the current reporting year. Collection contracts, scrap streams from cell factories and safe logistics can therefore be more valuable than speculative forecasts of near-term vehicle retirements. Recycling businesses face fixed operating costs even when the flows they expected have yet to arrive.

