European recovery targets remain in force after a 2026 review
On September 11, 2026, the European Commission concluded that existing battery recycling-efficiency and material-recovery targets remained appropriate. Under the EU Batteries Regulation, the 2030 recycling-efficiency thresholds include 80% for lead-acid and 70% for lithium-based batteries, measured by average weight. Recovery targets for 2027 require 90% for cobalt, copper, lead and nickel, and 50% for lithium; the latter target rises to 80% for 2031.

These regulatory benchmarks create a reason for collection networks and manufacturers to document incoming material and the quantity actually recovered. They do not establish that every recovery facility meets the targets or that sufficient feedstock will be available at any particular plant. For service providers, dependable measurement, material traceability and permitted processing routes become part of the offering, alongside the metal value obtained.

Different battery streams require different recovery economics
Lead-acid recycling is supported by longstanding replacement-battery collection systems, while lithium-ion recycling combines sorting, dismantling, thermal or mechanical preparation and downstream chemical separation. Rechargeable consumer cells, large stationary packs and electric-vehicle modules arrive in different forms and states of charge. Direct physical recovery, high-temperature treatments and hydrometallurgical techniques are consequently not interchangeable methods for every feedstock.

The product mix matters as much as plant size. Batteries with less nickel or cobalt can yield different metal revenues from nickel-rich cathodes, while some operators earn service fees for handling material. Safety controls, wastewater treatment and transport permissions affect operating margins. Recyclers may therefore need several validated routes rather than treating all discarded batteries as a single commodity supply.

