Companies deciding where to build processing capacity also need to distinguish the value of an incoming battery pack from the value that a recycler actually captures. Some contracts pay for safe treatment even when recovered-metal prices weaken; others depend heavily on chemical composition and the sale of refined outputs. The costs of discharging, transporting, dismantling and tracking batteries can be substantial. Careful comparison of service fees, recovery yields, collection distances and customers for secondary products is therefore essential before interpreting an attractive long-term market forecast as evidence that a particular recycling plant will be profitable.

